To address your request, here is the MATLAB code that implements a 2D source panel method. It reads panel points from a text file, prompts the user for flow conditions, and calculates pressure coefficients and lift coefficients using the Kutta-Joukowski theorem. It also checks the accuracy of the numerical solution for different panel numbers and compares results with experimental or analytical data.

```matlab
function sourcePanelMethod()
    % Read panel points from a text file
    panelPoints = readPanelPoints('panel_points.txt');

    % Ask user for flow conditions
    [Uinf, alpha] = getFlowConditions();

    % Calculate pressure coefficients and lift coefficient
    [cp, cl] = calculateCoefficients(panelPoints, Uinf, alpha);

    % Display pressure coefficients and lift coefficient
    disp('Pressure Coefficients:');
    disp(cp);
    disp(['Lift Coefficient: ', num2str(cl)]);

    % Compare with experimental or analytical results (example values)
    cl_analytical = 0.5; % Replace with actual analytical value
    disp(['Analytical Lift Coefficient: ', num2str(cl_analytical)]);
    disp(['Comparison: ', num2str(abs(cl - cl_analytical))]);

    % Check accuracy for different panel numbers
    panelNumbers = [10, 20, 30];
    for nPanels = panelNumbers
        refinedPanelPoints = refinePanels(panelPoints, nPanels + 1);
        [~, cl_n] = calculateCoefficients(refinedPanelPoints, Uinf, alpha);
        accuracy = abs(cl - cl_n) / cl;
        disp(['Accuracy with ', num2str(nPanels), ' panels: ', num2str(accuracy)]);
    end
end

function panelPoints = readPanelPoints(filename)
    % Reads panel points from a file where each line contains x y coordinates
    panelPoints = dlmread(filename);
end

function [Uinf, alpha] = getFlowConditions()
    % Prompts user for free stream velocity and angle of attack
    Uinf = input('Enter free stream velocity (m/s): ');
    alpha = input('Enter angle of attack (degrees): ');
    alpha = deg2rad(alpha);
end

function [cp, cl] = calculateCoefficients(panelPoints, Uinf, alpha)
    % Calculate pressure coefficients and lift coefficient
    nPanels = length(panelPoints) - 1;
    gamma = 4 * Uinf * sin(alpha); % Circulation for lifting flow

    for i = 1:nPanels
        x1 = panelPoints(i, 1);
        y1 = panelPoints(i, 2);
        x2 = panelPoints(i + 1, 1);
        y2 = panelPoints(i + 1, 2);
        midpoint_x = (x1 + x2) / 2;
        midpoint_y = (y1 + y2) / 2;
        cp_i = 1 - (gamma / (2 * pi * Uinf * sqrt((midpoint_x - x1)^2 + (midpoint_y - y1)^2)))^2;
        cp(i) = cp_i;
    end
    cl = gamma / Uinf; % Kutta-Joukowski theorem
end

function refinedPanelPoints = refinePanels(panelPoints, nPanels)
    % Refine the panels to have a different number of segments
    x = linspace(panelPoints(:, 1), nPanels);
    y = linspace(panelPoints(:, 2), nPanels);
    refinedPanelPoints = [x', y'];
end
```

This MATLAB script provides a framework for implementing a 2D source panel method. It reads panel points from a file, prompts the user for flow conditions, calculates pressure coefficients and lift coefficient using the Kutta-Joukowski theorem, checks the accuracy of the numerical solution for different panel numbers, and compares the calculated values with experimental or analytical results.

